# A bright black-and-white pencil sketch.
#
# The old darkroom trick, done in layers: desaturate, take a copy, invert it,
# blur it, and colour-dodge it back over the original. Dodge divides by the
# inverse of the blend, so anywhere the blurred copy matches its surroundings
# the result saturates to white paper — and only where it does *not*, which is
# where an edge is, does anything stay dark. Edges become graphite; flat areas
# become nothing.
#
# `blur` is the pencil: small is a hard line, large is a soft shaded stroke.
# `contrast` lifts the paper to white and pushes the strokes down toward black,
# so it is what "bright" is made of. Too much and the drawing goes sparse.

param blur = 12
param contrast = 0.32
param brightness = 0.03

adjust black-and-white
layer duplicate
adjust invert
filter gaussian-blur radius={blur}
set blend="Color Dodge"
layer flatten
adjust brightness-contrast contrast={contrast} brightness={brightness}
